  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A chain of length MMM is a graph on MMM vertices, such that its only edges are of the form (i,i+1)(i, i+1)(i,i+1) for each 1≤i&lt;M1 \\leq i \\lt M1≤i&lt;M.\n\nYou are given NNN chain graphs, each of length MMM.\n\nVertex jjj of the iii-th chain graph has the integer Ai,jA_{i, j}Ai,j​ written on it.\n\nYou would like to merge all these chains into a single tree of N⋅MN\\cdot MN⋅M vertices, by adding N−1N-1N−1 extra edges.\n\nWhat is the **minimum** possible diameter of such a tree?\n\nHere, the diameter of a tree is the maximum possible sum of values on the path connecting two vertices of the tree.\n\nFormally, for two vertices uuu and vvv, let f(u,v)f(u, v)f(u,v) denote the sum of values of all vertices that lie on the (unique) path from uuu to vvv. The diameter of the tree is then the maximum of f(u,v)f(u, v)f(u,v) across all pairs (u,v)(u, v)(u,v) of vertices.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM — the number of chains and the length of each chain.\nEach of the next NNN lines contains MMM space-separated integers. The iii-th of them contains Ai,1,Ai,2,…,Ai,MA_{i, 1}, A_{i, 2}, \\ldots, A_{i, M}Ai,1​,Ai,2​,…,Ai,M​ — the values of the vertices in the iii-th chain.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case print a single line containing one integer — the minimum possible diameter of the final tree.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "2≤N≤1052 \\leq N \\leq 10^52≤N≤105",
      "1≤M≤1051 \\leq M \\leq 10^51≤M≤105",
      "1≤Ai,j≤1091 \\leq A_{i,j} \\leq 10^91≤Ai,j​≤109",
      "The sum of N⋅MN\\cdot MN⋅M over all test cases won't exceed 5⋅1055\\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2 3\n2 3 1\n1 1 2\n3 3\n1 2 1\n4 3 3\n1 1 1\n2 1\n1\n5",
        "output": "8\n10\n6",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Connect the vertices (1,2)(1, 2)(1,2) and (2,2)(2, 2)(2,2) with an edge.\nThe diameter of the resulting tree is the path between (1,1)(1, 1)(1,1) and (2,3)(2, 3)(2,3), with a value of\nA1,1+A1,2+A2,2+A2,3=2+3+1+2=8A_{1,1} + A_{1,2} + A_{2,2} + A_{2,3} = 2+3+1+2 = 8A1,1​+A1,2​+A2,2​+A2,3​=2+3+1+2=8\nIt can be verified that this is optimal.\nTest case 222: Connect (1,2)(1, 2)(1,2) to (2,2)(2, 2)(2,2), and (2,2)(2, 2)(2,2) to (3,1)(3, 1)(3,1).\nThe diameter of the resulting tree is between (1,1)(1, 1)(1,1) and (2,1)(2, 1)(2,1), with a value of A1,1A_{1,1}A1,1​ + A1,2+A2,2+A2,1=1+2+3+4=10A_{1,2} + A_{2,2} + A_{2,1} = 1+2+3+4 = 10A1,2​+A2,2​+A2,1​=1+2+3+4=10.\nTest case 333: There are only two vertices, joining them gives a diameter of 1+5=61+5 = 61+5=6 which is clearly optimal."
      }
    ]
  },
